### 6. `indemnification_personal` — HIGH

**Rule:** A co-founder CMO faces personal liability exposure from: (a) regulatory actions (FDA enforcement), (b) third-party claims arising from the company's products, and (c) actions taken in their capacity as an officer. The agreement must specify that the company will indemnify the CMO for actions taken in good faith in their official capacity, and must define the scope (third-party claims only? regulatory actions? advancement of legal fees?). D&O insurance should be referenced.

**Triggers:** `indemnif`, `hold harmless`, `personal liability`, `defend`, `D&O`, `officer`, `director`, `regulatory`

**Red flags:**
- No indemnification clause at all
- Indemnification limited to third-party claims only (excludes regulatory)
- No advancement of legal fees provision
- No D&O insurance reference
- Indemnification conditioned on "final adjudication" (no interim protection)

**Draft template:**
```
INDEMNIFICATION. The Company shall indemnify, defend, and hold harmless [CMO NAME] from and against any and all claims, damages, losses, costs, and expenses (including reasonable attorneys' fees) arising out of or relating to [CMO NAME]'s actions taken in good faith in their capacity as Co-Founder and Chief Medical Officer of the Company, to the fullest extent permitted by applicable law. This indemnification shall include, without limitation: (a) third-party claims arising from the Company's products or services; (b) regulatory investigations or enforcement actions by the FDA or other governmental authorities, provided [CMO NAME] acted in good faith and in compliance with Company policy; and (c) advancement of legal fees and expenses prior to final adjudication, subject to [CMO NAME]'s undertaking to repay such amounts if it is ultimately determined that [CMO NAME] is not entitled to indemnification. The Company shall maintain Directors and Officers (D&O) liability insurance in an amount not less than $[X] per occurrence.
```

---

### 7. `deliverables_schedule_b` — HIGH

**Rule:** A co-founder agreement with deliverables must define: (a) whether vesting is time-based, deliverable-based, or hybrid, (b) acceptance criteria for each deliverable, (c) what happens if a deliverable is missed (cure period, equity forfeiture, termination), and (d) who has authority to accept or reject a deliverable. Ambiguity between time-based and deliverable-based vesting is the most common source of co-founder disputes.

**Triggers:** `deliverable`, `schedule B`, `milestone`, `time-based`, `performance`, `acceptance`, `full-time`

**Red flags:**
- "Deliverables" mentioned but Schedule B not attached or not defined
- No acceptance criteria (who decides if a deliverable is met?)
- No cure period for missed deliverables
- Hybrid vesting (time + deliverable) without defining which governs on conflict
- Full-time commitment required but no definition of "full-time"

**Draft template:**
```
DELIVERABLES. [CMO NAME]'s obligations are set forth in Schedule B attached hereto and incorporated by reference. Schedule B specifies: (a) each deliverable, (b) the due date or milestone trigger, (c) the acceptance criteria, and (d) the equity or compensation consequence of completion or non-completion. Deliverables shall be deemed accepted upon written confirmation from [CEO/CTO NAME] within [10] business days of submission. If [CEO/CTO NAME] does not respond within [10] business days, the deliverable shall be deemed accepted. If a deliverable is rejected, [CMO NAME] shall have [30] days to cure. If the deliverable is not cured within the cure period, the parties shall negotiate in good faith for [15] days before either party may invoke the dispute resolution provisions of Section [X].
```

---

### 8. `incorporation_entity_type` — HIGH

**Rule:** The choice between S-Corp and non-profit has direct consequences for the co-founder agreement: (a) S-Corps cannot have more than 100 shareholders, cannot have non-US shareholders, and cannot have multiple classes of stock — all of which affect equity structure; (b) non-profits cannot distribute equity to founders in the traditional sense — equity-like arrangements must use alternative structures (profits interest, phantom equity); (c) 83(b) elections apply to S-Corp restricted stock but not to non-profit arrangements. This must be resolved before signing.

**Triggers:** `S-Corp`, `S Corp`, `non-profit`, `nonprofit`, `501(c)`, `incorporation`, `entity`, `C-Corp`, `LLC`, `formation`

**Red flags:**
- Entity type not specified in the agreement
- S-Corp structure with multiple share classes (not permitted)
- Non-profit structure with traditional equity grant (not valid)
- 83(b) election referenced in a non-profit context
- Entity not yet incorporated at time of signing

**Governance trigger:** `incorporation_unresolved`

**Draft template:**
```
ENTITY TYPE. The Company is organized as a [S-Corporation / C-Corporation / Limited Liability Company] under the laws of the State of [Delaware / California / other]. The parties acknowledge that the equity structure set forth in this Agreement is contingent upon the Company's entity type and that certain provisions (including the 83(b) election in Section [X]) apply only to [S-Corporation / C-Corporation] structures. If the Company has not yet been incorporated as of the date of this Agreement, the parties agree to execute amended equity documentation within [30] days of incorporation that conforms to the entity type selected. [NOTE TO ATTORNEY: Resolve S-Corp vs. non-profit before signing. This is a blocking issue.]
```

---

### 9. `rspa_restricted_stock` — HIGH

**Rule:** The Restricted Stock Purchase Agreement (RSPA) is the legal vehicle for the equity grant. It must be executed simultaneously with the co-founder agreement. The RSPA defines the purchase price (typically par value, e.g., $0.0001/share), the repurchase right (company can buy back unvested shares at cost on termination), and the vesting schedule. Without a signed RSPA, the equity grant is not legally effective.

**Triggers:** `RSPA`, `restricted stock purchase`, `repurchase right`, `par value`, `stock purchase`, `equity grant`

**Red flags:**
- Co-founder agreement references equity but no RSPA is attached or referenced
- RSPA not executed simultaneously with the co-founder agreement
- No repurchase right defined
- Purchase price not specified
- Vesting schedule in co-founder agreement conflicts with RSPA

**Draft template:**
```
RSPA. Simultaneously with the execution of this Agreement, [CMO NAME] and the Company shall execute a Restricted Stock Purchase Agreement (the "RSPA") in the form attached hereto as Exhibit [B]. The RSPA shall govern the terms of the equity grant, including the purchase price, repurchase right, and vesting schedule. In the event of any conflict between this Agreement and the RSPA, the RSPA shall control with respect to the equity grant. The equity grant described in Section [X] of this Agreement shall not be effective until the RSPA is fully executed by both parties.
```

---

### 10. `mutual_deliverables` — MEDIUM

**Rule:** A co-founder agreement is bilateral. If the CMO has defined deliverables, the CEO/CTO must also have defined deliverables. An agreement that specifies CMO obligations in detail but leaves CEO/CTO obligations vague is one-sided and may be unenforceable or create leverage imbalance. Both parties' obligations should be in Schedule B.

**Triggers:** `mutual`, `CEO`, `CTO`, `co-founder`, `both parties`, `obligations`, `responsibilities`

**Red flags:**
- CMO deliverables defined but CEO/CTO deliverables absent or vague
- Only one party has a performance schedule
- "Best efforts" standard for one party but specific deliverables for the other

**Draft template:**
```
MUTUAL OBLIGATIONS. The obligations of each Co-Founder are set forth in Schedule B. [CEO/CTO NAME]'s obligations include, without limitation: [list specific deliverables — e.g., product development milestones, fundraising targets, operational responsibilities]. [CMO NAME]'s obligations include, without limitation: [list specific deliverables — e.g., clinical protocol development, regulatory strategy, medical advisory board formation]. Both Co-Founders acknowledge that their respective obligations are material to this Agreement and that failure to perform may constitute grounds for the remedies set forth in Section [X].
```

